WEBVTT

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.040
I'm trying to have a shave, not a shower.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.630
You're in my way.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.870
best nightie? I don't remember

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.040
seeing that before. Not in the daylight, anyway.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.390
The one I bought for

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.120
our honeymoon. You know, with a lovely

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.260
pair of matching panties. Oh, yeah, we must

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.340
unpack those one of these days.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.350
But, if you

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.230
wouldn't mind... Look, Anne,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.930
just a minute.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.990
I know you haven't been married very long,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.000
and living with a member of the opposite sex is a new

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.020
experience for you giving you the benefit of the doubt of course but didn't your mother tell you

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.840
the full facts of life like a man having to shave every morning my mother has nothing at all to do

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.070
with the facts of life yeah i bet she never gets a chance well i'm fed up with living in this one

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:53.100
tiny room like a pair of rabbits cooped up in a hutch now i know how they feel don't stop them

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:59.680
breeding though does it that encourages them you don't need any encouragement oh get off

00:01:59.680 --> 00:01:59.920
Thank you.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.940
Here, put your shirt on before you get carried away again.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.880
After all your promises, you make me live in a place like this.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.590
Oh, if ever my mum saw what I've come down to.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.170
She warned me and she was dead right.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.970
Yeah, your mother's always warning people.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.960
And she's always dead right.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.000
The Clive Jenkins of the mother's union, she is.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.250
If she saw this place, she'd drop dead.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:30.000
Well, since you put it like that, I don't know whether to invite you.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.690
right around or not. How can you say that? Never mind, love. Here, come on. I must be

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:41.120
off. I'll be late. Late? Late for what? Work. What work? Signing on at the Labour

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:48.230
Exchange. Well, they find your work, don't they? What you put yourself down as? Oh,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:55.750
yes, that's a bit tricky. Well, what? Tomato picker. Tomatoes? This time of the year?

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:00.000
But they only grow in the Canary Islands. Well, I'm willing to travel. Oh,

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:08.460
hang on have you got your shovel why we've had a caller in the night that whip it from next door

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:15.790
left his visiting card on the step oh no not again oh come on love don't let it get you down

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.620
it's supposed to be lucky if they do it on your doorstep you might win the pause come on cheer up

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:30.000
give us a smile that's better oh Bert it's just this place it's not much of a start well think

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.750
born in a jungle. Only thing he had between them was a pair of fig leaves. As I recall,

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:41.550
they were being fully provided for by the Almighty. So are we. Eh? Who? Well, nowadays

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:48.420
they call it the National Assistance. Don't daft. Anyway, I mean, I must stand more chance

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.420
than Adam. The only job he could get down at the Labour Exchange would be picking apples.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.760
Here, Bert. What? Bring back something decent for dinner. Yeah, what with? Well, if you're

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.000
going down the Labour, you'll be able to get your Social Security money. Use that.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.550
Blimey, I haven't got it yet and you're spending it already.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:04.770
Oh, hello.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:05.890
We're going to have a visitor.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.950
See to the end of your sands if it's laid another egg.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:16.790
I can't stand her.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:17.810
Tell her I'm out.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:18.350
Too late.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.310
The old boot's here.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:20.830
Hello, sunshine.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.040
Time some people was at work.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.130
Morning, dear.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.910
Hope I'm not interrupting anything.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:27.880
I thought I'd wait till you was up.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.640
Very tactful.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:44.010
was never one for putting me foot in it if you ask me you have been on your

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:52.150
stick but it was your whip it Bob I love don't climb on my way excuse me I love

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.650
grind you getting used to living in a caravan

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.000
yet I've already told you we're only living here temporarily that's what

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.990
what you said, when we come here, that was

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.530
15 years ago.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.970
Here, just think, if I'd have

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.950
had any sense, I could have murdered my old

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.950
man, gone to prison, I'd be

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.790
coming out now all fancy free.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.720
You don't realise these things at the

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:14.780
time, though, do you?

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.090
Well, Mrs Briggs, I do have to

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.790
get on. I've got a load of washing to do.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.270
I almost forgot what I come over

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.270
here for. This letter

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.510
came for you yesterday.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:27.590
Or was it the day before?

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.000
Here, it's

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.050
Oh, that was a genuine mistake, love.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:35.750
You see, I couldn't find my Wally's reading glasses.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:39.510
But as soon as I see it was for you, I sealed it up again right away.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:40.430
Good.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:41.430
It's from your mother.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.430
She's coming to tea today.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:45.810
What?

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.010
Bloody nerve.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.970
You've no right to have opened it, damn cheek.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.190
That's what comes of living in this dump with people like you around.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.190
Oh, proper little madam, ain't ya?

00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:00.000
Right, pack of lies you've been telling your mother, telling her you're living in one

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.440
posh mobile homes on a luxury site.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.060
Oh, shut your mouth.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.920
All right, you wait your old girl gets here.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.380
I know what you've told her.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.350
Dear Mummy, do come to Din Din's

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.310
at our stadium.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.470
It's quite easy to find.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.290
Just turn left at the knacker's yard and go straight forward.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.290
Come on in, my little angel.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.170
There's a beauty.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:25.990
Let's the wave from you.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:27.370
Hello, I.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:28.610
Feed in the chicken, then.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.000
Now, teaching.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.920
at her dance, mate.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.870
Of course I'm feeding her.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.460
She loves this stuff.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.060
Corn on the cob. Gets through several a day

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.090
if I let her. Yeah, listen, Wally, you couldn't lend me

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.910
a few quid your Friday, could you? You've been down the

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.090
betting shop again then. Well, I couldn't hang about

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.090
that labour exchange all day long with them layabouts.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.390
Anyway, I had this red-hot tip.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.360
What do we go with you then? Clark

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:53.160
at the labour exchange.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.260
Useless lot. Yes, that's right.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.340
Come on, come on, Flory,

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:00.000
love. You are a beauty.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.890
ain't ya?

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.720
Come on, Flurry love, you are a beauty, ain't ya?

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.720
Why the hell don't you get in there with her?

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.570
She's a better looking bird than you are.

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:16.360
See you later, mate.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:17.360
You're right.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.740
Oi, Jonesy.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.740
Oi, I've got a phone to pick with you.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:24.740
What's the matter?

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:29.460
This old clock you sold me, it keeps stopping, it's not worth having.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.300
- That's a genuine antique, that was a gift for a father.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.540
- It still won't go.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:38.920
- Well, that's because you don't know where to bash it.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.260
There you are, go for days now.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.620
- Right, if it stops again, I'll bring it across to you.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.300
- Yeah, right, oh no, no, no, no, don't do that,

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.800
because my wife doesn't know I got it for you,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.100
and, well, if she saw that, she'd want one like it,

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.020
and I'm a bit skint at the moment.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.870
- Well, that's nothing unusual, is it?

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.290
Come on, Wally, we gotta go.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:55.010
- All right, my love.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:58.630
Bye bye, Flory, I'll be back this evening.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:00.000
She loves me, that bird.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:02.660
Hello, Betty Love.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:03.720
Oh, you're home early.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:05.150
How'd you get on at the labour?

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:06.010
Oh, great smashing.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:06.790
Did you?

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:07.690
That's wonderful.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:08.450
What happened?

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:09.150
I've got no jobs.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:10.520
Oh, Bert.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.090
Did you get your social security money?

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:13.530
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:14.770
Oh, well, that's something.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.180
Yeah, what you got all that junk out for?

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.120
These happen to be my mother's wedding presents.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.680
I couldn't leave them in the suitcase forever, Bert.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:26.300
Oh, and that reminds me, where's that old antique clock she gave us?

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.690
The one you took to have repaired?

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:29.330
Oh, the antique clock.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.000
Yeah, but...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.980
Oh, it's still in the shop. I think they've sent off to the factory for a spare part.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.510
Spare part? Well, it's over a hundred years old.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.260
Yeah, well, that's why it's been so long coming.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.050
Well, I mean, they probably had to make one special.

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:44.990
Anyway, what do you get all this old junk out for?

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.430
Well, it's not as if the old bag was coming to see us, is it?

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.450
I'm wrong, aren't I?

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.160
She's coming this evening.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:56.880
You're joking.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:57.600
I'm not.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:58.970
Well, what do you want to invite her for?

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:00.000
Oh, I didn't, Bert.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.590
I tried to put her off, love.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.960
I shouldn't have rented a caravan.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.450
I should have rented a submarine.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.870
At least we could have submerged.

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.510
Well, it's not my fault, Bert.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:15.400
Anyway, she's coming to dinner.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.260
Dinner?

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.260
And what's wrong with that?

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:18.170
Well, the way she eats.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.340
It's like inviting Dracula to a blood bank.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.550
What you get for dinner?

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.020
Oh, yeah.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.240
Come on, come on.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:42.770
Rolls and a bag of crisps.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:44.990
I can't give that to my mum.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.430
Yeah, no, of course you can't.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:47.510
Shall I get another sausage roll?

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:51.950
I can see we're going to have a real right blowout on that lot.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.710
You all dole money, didn't you?

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:56.570
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I told you.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.090
All right then, Bert, go on out.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.790
Go and get something decent for dinner.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.220
- Yeah, but you see, go hang on a minute... - No, Bert, go on, no bucks, look, come on, here's your coat.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:07.540
- What? - And I'll try and make this place look respectable.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.340
- Oh, and Bert, bring back something to drink with dinner. - What?

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.000
- Some wine, come on, you know, you've got all that social security money.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.430
- Buy wine on the social security? That's immoral, that is.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.520
- But being immoral's never worried you before.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.300
Oh, and I shall need some veg.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:27.860
I don't know how I'm going to manage. I haven't got enough to make a decent meal.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.800
- You're not the only one.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.180
Shut up. Don't you start.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:52.130
All right, Betty, love.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.270
Where have you been, Bert? You've been hours.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:56.750
Yeah, well, I had a bit of trouble finding exactly the right thing.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:57.590
Did you get everything?

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:58.430
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.500
a bottle of wine. Look at that. Oh, lovely. Box of chocks for your mum. Oh, Bert, that

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:12.810
is nice. What do you get for the meal? For your mother, the best. A lovely, plump, juicy

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:22.970
chicken. Raving mad? What the hell did you buy that for? Why didn't you get me a proper

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:28.920
one from the supermarket, ready for cooking? From the supermarket? For your mother? That's

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.000
a farm fresh bird.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.280
Yeah, look, I've got some vegetables to go with it.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:40.820
Bird?

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:41.520
What are you talking about?

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:42.780
That's much better than the birds you get.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:43.520
That's far and fresh.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.440
How do you know?

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.360
How can you be sure that's fresh?

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:47.910
You don't worry, I know.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:50.230
It's ridiculous.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.010
It must have cost you twice as much as the ones I get.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:53.850
No, no, no.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.030
It was very reasonable, really.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:58.010
And I suppose you went miles and miles just to get it?

00:11:58.590 --> 00:11:59.940
No, not far at all, really.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:06.240
Well, I'm sorry, Bert. I can't manage that thing. You'll have to take it back.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:07.240
No, I can't take it back.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.240
Why not? You haven't done anything to it.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:13.320
I just can't take it back, that's all.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.860
Bert, what do I have to do? I mean, I don't know how to prepare it.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:18.860
Well, you just pluck it.

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:20.990
How? How do I pluck it?

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.070
Well, you pluck your eyebrows, don't you?

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:27.620
Jesus!

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:39.370
Don't be silly. All you have to do is you just, you get my pliers out of that drawer, will you?

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:43.720
I'll ask Mr. Briggs. He knows all about chickens.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.700
Oh, no, no, no. You mustn't do that. I mustn't let you.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:48.320
Why not?

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:49.720
Well, he's gone out for the day.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:50.480
Are you sure?

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.020
I made sure.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.120
Oh, no, no, yes. Oh, yes, I'm sure.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.060
Look, come on, listen. I'll tell you what, look.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:57.740
You pull one end, I'll pull the other. We'll meet in the middle.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:33.470
I still say you should have brought a proper chicken.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:35.430
You know, in a polythene bag.

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:38.500
Proper chickens don't come in polythene bags.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.270
They grow up with feathers on, not plastic macs.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:47.120
I've seen so many feathers in all my life.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:48.900
Yeah, come off it.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:51.300
What about that weekend in Brighton when we split the mattress?

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:57.940
Look, open the door and I'll dump this lot outside.

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:00.000
Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.090
Don't do that. I can't let you.

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.430
- Why not? - Well, it's polluting the countryside.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.890
Well, you've just tipped the potato peelings out of the window.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:10.370
Yeah, I know, but feathers is different. Besides, it might come in handy.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.080
- Handy for what? - Stuffing.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.660
Oh, you're not suggesting I'll bang it back in the bird?

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:20.640
Don't be silly. I mean, for cushions and things like that, I'll put them in here.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:27.310
I can't get these tiny feathers off around the neck. I just can't get hold of them.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.410
Yeah, hang on. Oh, I know.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:40.080
I'll spoil it!

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:42.680
Blimey, if it does your legs, it should be able to cope with this lot.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.230
Come in.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:49.300
Here.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:50.320
Use yours.

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:56.430
There you are.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.430
That's working a treat.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:00.000
Mind you, it's making his neck a bit sore.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:08.840
